INT. COUNTY PAROLE OFFICE - WAITING AREA - DAY

Rain taps the high windows. A fluorescent tube flickers over a row of orange plastic chairs. The radiator ticks. Somewhere a vending machine hums to itself.

Marcus Doyle, 30s, sits with his hands between his knees, a manila folder resting in his lap. Across from him, Rowan Pierce, 20s, slouches in a chair, scrolling a cracked phone. Rowan uses they/them pronouns and wears a battered denim jacket.

ROWAN
(not looking up)
You're early.

MARCUS
Habit. They count it against you if you're late.

ROWAN
Everybody counts everything against you.

The fluorescent tube buzzes, dims, steadies. Wind pushes at the glass.

ANGLE ON the reception desk. MRS. HALE, 60s, sits behind a wall of plexiglass, glasses low on her nose, stamping a stack of forms.

MRS. HALE
Doyle. Marcus. You're up in five. Pierce, you're after.

MARCUS
Thank you, ma'am.

ROWAN
(muttering)
Ma'am. Jesus.

The intercom on the wall CRACKLES. A man's voice, thin and wrong.

OFFICER VANCE (V.O.)
Hale, send Doyle back now. Skip the line.

MRS. HALE
(leaning to the intercom)
Officer Vance, he's not on until--

OFFICER VANCE (V.O.)
Now.

Mrs. Hale hesitates. The tube overhead goes out entirely. The room is grey and green with rain-light.

MARCUS
(rising slowly)
That's not his voice.

ROWAN
(finally looking up)
What do you mean it's not his voice?

MARCUS
Vance has a limp in his voice. I mean he whistles on his s's. That was somebody doing him.

CLOSE ON the intercom's red light. It blinks. Once. Twice.

OFFICER VANCE (V.O.)
Doyle. Don't make me ask again.

ROWAN
(standing, voice low)
Don't go.

MARCUS
I have to. If I don't show, it's a violation.

ROWAN
It's a trap.

MARCUS
It's always a trap.

WE SEE the hallway door behind Mrs. Hale. It is open an inch. Beyond it, only darkness.

MRS. HALE
(very quietly, not looking at either of them)
Don't go in there, honey.

INSERT - the desk calendar. Today's date is circled in red. Under it, in handwriting: "THEY COME BACK."

MARCUS
(reading it)
Who wrote that?

MRS. HALE
(a thin, brittle smile)
I did. Thirty years ago.

The radiator stops ticking. The rain stops. Even the vending machine goes silent.

ROWAN
(whispering)
Marcus. Look at the window.

BACK TO SCENE.

Reflected in the dark glass: the three of them, and a fourth shape standing in the open hallway door behind them, tall and still.

OFFICER VANCE (V.O.)
(from the intercom, from the hallway, from everywhere)
Doyle. Pierce. Hale. Come in. Come in. Come in.
